\title[Hyperbolic--Pythagorean Geodesics]{Hyperbolic--Pythagorean Geodesics:\\ The Berggren Tree in the Poincar\'e Half-Plane}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{7 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We construct a dictionary between the Berggren ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples and the Poincar\'e upper half-plane $\HH$, and use it to determine the metric geometry of that tree exactly. Writing a primitive triple in Euclid coordinates $(m,n)$, so that $(a,b,c)=(m^2-n^2,2mn,m^2+n^2)$, we attach the half-plane point $z(m,n)=(n+i)/m$. The central identity is
\[
  \cosh \dH\bigl(i, z(m,n)\bigr) \;=\; \frac{m^2+n^2+1}{2m},
\]
in which the hypotenuse of the triple appears as the numerator of a hyperbolic cosine. From it we deduce a sharp logarithmic trajectory law $\tfrac12\log c \le \dH(i,z(m,n)) \le \tfrac12\log(2(c+1))$; a complete description of the residual $\resid = \dH - \tfrac12\log c$ as the slope function $\tfrac12\log(1+(n/m)^2)$ plus a gap pinned between $n^2/c^2$ and $n^2/(c(c-1))$; exact monotonicity of the residual along all three Berggren branches, unconditional for two of them and governed by the threshold $m^2 = 2mn+n^2$ for the third, including a Pell boundary layer $(m-n)^2 = 2n^2+1$ on which the real-variable argument genuinely fails; the tree structure of the depth function, with $\depth \gtrsim \dH$ but no reverse inequality; and volume growth $\#\mathcal{B}(R) = \Theta(e^{2R})$. The last of these yields a no-free-lunch theorem: although two representations of $N$ as a sum of two squares split $N$ completely by Euler's method, and although colliding nodes are hyperbolic neighbours, the ball guaranteed to contain a collision for $N$ already contains $\asymp N$ nodes.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, Poincar\'e half-plane, hyperbolic geometry, Euler factorization, Pell equation, volume growth}

